## Configuration

Settings live in named profiles in `{{ profile_file }}`. `{{ cli_name }} config init`
creates the file with an empty `default` profile, and any command creates it on
first run if it is missing. `{{ cli_name }} config show` lists a profile with the
defaults filled in, `{{ cli_name }} config set --param <setting> --value <value>`
changes one setting and `--profile <name>` on any command selects a profile other
than `default`.

| Setting | Default | Effect |
|---|---|---|
{% for key, default, text in profile_settings %}
| `{{ key }}` | {{ 'unset' if default is none else '`' ~ (default | string | lower if default is sameas true or default is sameas false else default) ~ '`' }} | {{ text }} |
{% endfor %}

Each setting is resolved through four layers, highest precedence first:

1. The command-line flag, for the settings that have one (`--base-url`).
2. The environment variable `{{ package_name | upper }}_<SETTING>`, the setting
   name in upper snake case: `baseUrl` reads `{{ package_name | upper }}_BASE_URL`.
3. The value stored in the profile selected with `--profile`, or `default`.
4. The built-in default.

## Authentication

Requests are authenticated with a bearer token: when `accessToken` is set, every
request carries an `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header. Without it, requests
are sent anonymously. Obtaining the token is up to the API; once you have it,
store it in the profile:

```bash
{{ cli_name }} config set --param accessToken --value <token>
```

or hand it to a single invocation through the environment:

```bash
{{ package_name | upper }}_ACCESS_TOKEN=<token> {{ cli_name }} {% if first_group %}{{ first_group.cli_name }} {{ first_group.operations[0].command_name }}{% else %}config show{% endif %}

```

The profile file is created readable by its owner only, and `--debug` output
redacts the token.

